  "query_preview": "/SATURDAY THROUGH WEDNESDAY/...\nIssued at 115 AM MDT Thu Oct 15 2026\n\nThe Intermountain west starts to take a temporary break from \nthe active weather pattern starting Saturday morning. A slow \nmoving ridge starts to set up and meander every so slightly \ntowards the central plains. The region will gradually warm up \ndue to this ridge but don't expect any summer like temperatures \nfor the rest of the season. The global models have 700mb \ntemperatures only warming up to between 6-10c through the next \nweek after this freeze from a visit from a polar airmass. With \nthe sun angle slowing dropping I'd wager that 80 degree days are\nprobably done for this forecast area. Instead the greater \ngamble would be on wearing shorts or pants in the morning as \ntemperatures start out in the 30's and 40's and climbing into \nthe 60's and 70's by the afternoon. By the mid-week another low \npressure system is progged to slide down the west coast as \nanother low is just hanging around the east coast to put the \nIntermountain West on the forward edge of an Omega block \ncentered over the central plains. The low off the west coast \nhowever will be a critical player to our regions forecast. The \ncyclonic rotation will be feeding moisture into our region to \nkeep the afternoon RH values high enough to not present any \ncurrent fire weather concerns while also stacking the cards on \nany needed precipitation reaching the ground as multiple \nshortwaves move past the Rockies for some precipitation chances \nlate next week.",
